"# The sandbox is corrupting JPEG data regardless. Let me check the actual JPEG data through Python's open() write\n# The corruption appears to be at curl-output level when piped anywhere\n# Try: use curl with --output that doesn't go through pipe\n\n# Actually, let me try using 'dd' or write directly via Python \n# But Python has socket issues. Let me check if there's a way to do this with the shell builtin \n\n# Try: use curl --output-binary or raw mode\ncurl --help all 2>&1 | grep -iE \"raw|binary|encode|hex|file-out\" | head -10\necho \"---\"\n# Try --output - with hex encoding via shell\ncurl -sL -A \"Mozilla/5.0\" -e \"http://ppbc.iplant.cn/sp/29482\" \"https://img2.iplant.cn/image2/0/33BCF56261CA3EA9.jpg\" --output - 2>&1 | wc -c\necho \"---\"\n# Write the hex output (which IS ASCII) directly, then convert back\ncurl -sL -A \"Mozilla/5.0\" -e \"http://ppbc.iplant.cn/sp/29482\" \"https://img2.iplant.cn/image2/0/33BCF56261CA3EA9.jpg\" --output - 2>&1 | xxd -p > /tmp/img.hex\nls -la /tmp/img.hex\nxxd /tmp/img.hex 2>&1 | head -2\necho \"Hex file size (chars):\"\nwc -c /tmp/img.hex\necho \"Expected if 200KB binary: ~400KB hex\"\necho \"---\"\n# Now decode it back to binary\nxxd -r -p /tmp/img.hex > /tmp/img_fromhex.jpg\nls -la /tmp/img_fromhex.jpg\nxxd /tmp/img_fromhex.jpg 2>&1 | head -3"
